@*
    PasswordField — password input with a show/hide toggle on the right.

    Presentation only. Extracted (Sprint 6.5, 2026-08-14) because the nine password inputs in the
    app were split across two primitives — FluentTextField with TextFieldType.Password on the auth
    and profile screens, a raw <input type="password"> in the user-create drawer — so adding a
    toggle at each site would have meant maintaining the behaviour twice. This uses a plain <input>
    for both: FluentTextField owns its own internal input element, which makes overlaying a control
    inside its border fragile across Fluent versions.

    Security note: the revealed state is deliberately NOT persisted and resets whenever the
    component is re-rendered from a closed surface, so a password never comes back visible on a
    drawer or page the user reopens.

    Public API
      Value          string           Two-way bound; use @bind-Value.
      Id             string?          Forwarded to the input for <label for="...">.
      Placeholder    string?
      Disabled       bool
      AutoComplete   string?          e.g. "current-password", "new-password". Default "off".

    Usage
      <PasswordField Id="password" @bind-Value="_password" Disabled="_busy" AutoComplete="current-password" />
*@

<div class="pwfield">
    <input id="@Id"
           type="@(_revealed ? "text" : "password")"
           class="pwfield-input"
           value="@Value"
           placeholder="@Placeholder"
           disabled="@Disabled"
           autocomplete="@(AutoComplete ?? "off")"
           @oninput="OnInput" />
    @*
        aria-pressed is formatted to a lowercase string, not bound to the bool directly. Blazor
        renders a bool attribute value by omitting the attribute when false and emitting it with an
        empty value when true, so `aria-pressed="@_revealed"` never produces a valid true/false and
        the control is not announced as a toggle at all. Same idiom as NavRail's aria-expanded.
    *@
    <button type="button"
            class="pwfield-toggle"
            disabled="@Disabled"
            aria-pressed="@_revealed.ToString().ToLowerInvariant()"
            aria-label="@(_revealed ? "Hide password" : "Show password")"
            title="@(_revealed ? "Hide password" : "Show password")"
            @onclick="Toggle">
        <FluentIcon Value="@(_revealed
            ? new Icons.Regular.Size16.EyeOff()
            : (Icon)new Icons.Regular.Size16.Eye())" aria-hidden="true" />
    </button>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter] public string Value { get; set; } = "";
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<string> ValueChanged { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public string? Id { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public string? Placeholder { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public bool Disabled { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public string? AutoComplete { get; set; }

    private bool _revealed;

    private Task OnInput(ChangeEventArgs e) =>
        ValueChanged.InvokeAsync(e.Value?.ToString() ?? "");

    // Click-to-toggle only — no press-and-hold. The toggle is deliberately in the tab order: it was
    // briefly tabindex="-1" to keep a sign-in form tabbing straight from the password to the submit
    // button, but that made revealing a password impossible without a mouse, which defeats the
    // control. It already sits after the input in DOM order, so it costs one tab stop.
    private void Toggle() => _revealed = !_revealed;
}
